Here's a Java solution to the problem using logarithms. This solution works by taking the logarithm base 3 of the number `n`. If the result is an integer, then `n` is a power of 3.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        return Math.log(n) / Math.log(3) % 1 == 0;
    }
}
```

However, the above solution might have a slight precision issue due to the limitations of floating-point arithmetic in Java. A more robust solution would be to use the `Math.log10` method and compare the result with the logarithm of the largest power of 3 that doesn't exceed `n`. Here's how you can do it:

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        double log = Math.log(n) / Math.log(3);
        return log == Math.floor(log);
    }
}
```

Or, you can use the `Math.pow` function to directly check if `n` is a power of 3:

